Let’s choose BugTime: an AI debugging platform that traces a bug from its symptom to the commit that introduced it, then proposes and tests a fix.

The signature demo: submit a broken login flow → reproduce the failure → identify the breaking commit → generate a patch → show passing tests.

One stack detail: Web3.js is an Ethereum integration library, and it was sunset in March 2025. I’ll include it as an optional report-verification module, since you requested it. The main interface will use React/Next.js. 

What the product does
A developer connects a repository and submits a bug description, stack trace, or failing test. BugTime investigates in an isolated environment and returns:

A reproducible failure, when possible.
Relevant files and code references.
The likely breaking commit, with evidence.
A proposed patch and regression test.
Test results and a downloadable investigation report.
It must distinguish “suspected cause” from “verified cause.” If it cannot reproduce a bug or reliably identify the commit, the interface should show that clearly.

The user journey
Add a repository: GitHub URL, branch, and supported test command.
Describe the bug: expected behavior, actual behavior, and optional logs.
Start investigation: follow progress and inspect retrieved evidence.
Review findings: reproduction result, likely cause, and commit history.
Review the patch: compare code changes and test results.
Export: download the patch/report; later, create a PR after approval.
How RAG, orchestration, and fine-tuning fit
Component	Its actual job
RAG	Retrieve relevant functions, tests, documentation, issues, and commit diffs. Combine semantic search with exact symbol/text search.
Orchestrator	Decide which investigation step runs next, manage retries, track evidence, and stop when the budget is exhausted.
Fine-tuning	Train a smaller model to rank likely relevant files from a bug description, using historical issue-to-fix examples.
Execution tools	Run Git commands, reproduce failures, apply patches, and execute tests inside isolated containers.
Fine-tuning comes after a working baseline. We’ll compare file-ranking quality and investigation success before and after training, using held-out repositories to reduce data leakage.

Investigation workflow
Stage	Action	Evidence produced
Intake	Parse the report and repository setup	Structured bug description
Retrieval	Find relevant code and history	File references and candidate commits
Reproduction	Create or run a failing test	Failure output
Commit investigation	Test candidate commits; use Git bisect when a reliable good/bad test exists	Suspected or verified breaking commit
Repair	Generate a focused patch and regression test	Reviewable diff
Verification	Run the reproduction test and relevant existing tests	Results, failures, and limitations
Review	Assemble findings for the developer	Investigation report
Use LangGraph to coordinate this workflow and persist checkpoints so interrupted investigations can resume. Its documentation supports checkpoint-based persistence. 

UI direction
A professional developer workspace: moderately detailed, readable, and restrained.

Think familiar repository navigation, a useful investigation dashboard, and a clear code-review workspace.

Screen	Main content
Dashboard	Repository list, recent investigations, status, and “New investigation” button
Repository	Branch, indexing status, detected framework, test configuration
New investigation	Bug description, expected/actual behavior, logs, branch selection
Investigation workspace	Progress timeline, findings, evidence, logs, and patch
History	Searchable previous runs with outcomes and duration
Settings	Model configuration, execution limits, integrations
Investigation workspace layout:

Left sidebar: repository navigation and previous runs.
Main area: findings with tabs for Overview, Evidence, Changes, and Tests.
Right panel: current step, elapsed time, and execution budget.
Expandable bottom panel: terminal output.
Visual choices:

Slate backgrounds, slightly lighter panels, blue primary actions.
Green for passing tests, amber for uncertainty, red for failures.
Readable typography; monospace for code and logs.
Subtle borders and short transitions.
Proper empty, loading, partial-result, and error states.
Light/dark mode, with dark mode as the initial default.
Web3.js integration
An optional “Verify report” feature:

Generate a hash of the finalized investigation report.
Let the user record that hash through a small smart contract.
Later, compare a report’s hash against the recorded value.
Keep source code and logs off-chain. This proves that a report matches the recorded version; it does not prove that the diagnosis is correct.

Start on a local development chain. Wallet connection appears only inside this feature, so ordinary debugging stays straightforward. Because Web3.js is sunset, keep this integration isolated and replaceable.

Proposed stack
Layer	Choice
Frontend	Next.js, React, TypeScript
UI	Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Monaco diff editor
Backend	FastAPI
Orchestration	LangGraph
Retrieval	Qdrant + exact code search + Git history
Database	PostgreSQL
Execution	Isolated Docker workers with resource/time limits
Live progress	Server-Sent Events
Fine-tuning	Hugging Face PEFT/LoRA
Blockchain module	Web3.js + small Solidity contract
Cursor is our development environment for implementing these pieces. The deployed application runs its own backend, models, and workers.

Build priorities
Priority	Deliverable	Completion check
P0 — Working foundation	UI, repository import, indexing, investigation records	A supported repository loads and relevant code can be retrieved
P1 — Bug reproduction	Sandbox runner, failing-test capture, live progress	A seeded bug is reproduced with visible evidence
P2 — Commit investigation	History retrieval and conditional Git bisect	A known regression is traced to its introducing commit
P3 — Repair and verification	Patch generation, regression tests, diff review	The failure test passes after the patch and relevant tests remain green
P4 — Fine-tuning	Training dataset, file-ranking adapter, evaluation	Measured comparison against the baseline
P5 — Integrations	PR creation and Web3.js report verification	Reviewed patch can be exported; report hash can be verified
First release scope: Python repositories using pytest, one repository per investigation, and a bounded number of repair attempts. That gives us a complete, testable workflow before expanding to JavaScript, monorepos, and multiple services.

Our first build target is P0: the dashboard, repository setup, investigation form, and evidence workspace backed by real repository indexing.